3. Viability Theory: Precise Statement

3.1 Deterministic Controlled Viability

For the controlled system:

\dot z=f(z,u,\theta,\omega), \qquad u\in U,

define the set-valued map:

F(z)=\{f(z,u,\theta,\omega):u\in U\}.

Viability Theorem (Aubin, 1991). Let F be a Marchaud map. A closed set K is viable under F if and only if K is a viability domain:

> \forall z\in K,\qquad F(z)\cap T_K(z)\neq\varnothing,
> 

where T_K(z) is the contingent cone to K at z.

For the benchmark, the condition to verify is:

\boxed{
\forall z\in K_{\rm service},\qquad
\{f(z,u,\theta,\omega):u\in U\}\cap T_{K_{\rm service}}(z)\neq\varnothing
}

under the specified uncertainty model.

3.2 Separation of Deterministic and Stochastic Statements

The deterministic tangency condition is not sufficient for the stochastic chance-constrained problem. The two formulations must be kept conceptually distinct:

· Viability theory characterizes deterministic existence of viable trajectories.
· Chance-constrained safety characterizes probabilistic confinement over the horizon.

Validation status: The separation is theoretically correct and necessary. Viability theory operates on deterministic set-valued maps. Stochastic viability theory is a distinct framework defining "maximum viability probability" under uncertainty. The two are not interchangeable.

