6. Multicaloric Coupling: Coupling \neq Synergy

6.1 Second-Order Expansion

For small field increments:

\Delta S
=
\sum_i
\frac{\partial S}{\partial X_i}\Delta X_i
+
\frac12
\sum_{i,j}
\frac{\partial^2S}{\partial X_i\partial X_j}
\Delta X_i\Delta X_j
+
O(\|\Delta X\|^3).

The cross-susceptibility:

\chi_{ij}=\frac{\partial^2S}{\partial X_i\partial X_j}

characterizes coupling. It does not, by itself, imply beneficial synergy.

6.2 System-Level Synergy

\boxed{
\Delta J_{\rm sys}
=
J(\mathcal A_i)
+
J(\mathcal A_j)
-
J(\mathcal A_{ij}).
}

· \Delta J_{\rm sys}>0: system-level synergy.
· \Delta J_{\rm sys}=0: independence.
· \Delta J_{\rm sys}<0: incompatibility.

Validation status: The separation of coupling from synergy is directly validated. A July 2025 Physical Review B paper provides the first experimental evaluation of cross-correlation-derived entropy change in a magnetoelectric compound, developing a thermodynamic framework for the multicaloric effect. The paper explicitly distinguishes the cross-correlation term \chi_{12} from simple coexistence of monocaloric effects. The system-level synergy metric \Delta J_{\rm sys} has no direct precedent and is a proposed definition.

