15. Scientific Contribution

Biological preservation is formulated as a constrained dynamical-system synthesis problem rather than as an intrinsically refrigeration problem. A biological material is represented by a partially observed, stochastic state vector whose evolution depends on thermal, atmospheric, biological, and material processes. Preservation is defined by confinement of this state, together with cumulative degradation variables, within a viability region over a finite operating horizon.

Thermal refrigeration is therefore treated as one admissible actuator family within a larger architecture space containing active refrigeration, caloric effects, radiative and evaporative heat rejection, thermal storage, atmospheric control, and distributed sensing and control.

The synthesis problem is to identify physically realizable architectures and policies that satisfy identical preservation, robustness, observability, resource, and maturity constraints while minimizing a vector of thermodynamic, biological, and engineering costs.

No alternative mechanism is assumed superior to conventional vapor-compression refrigeration. Superiority is defined operationally through Pareto dominance under a pre-registered adversarial benchmark. The conventional refrigerator is therefore an incumbent architecture, not a protected optimum.

The framework is designed to determine, under explicitly specified physical, biological, uncertainty, and resource constraints, whether an alternative preservation architecture can Pareto-dominate the incumbent vapor-compression architecture.

---

16. Final Statement

The refrigerator is neither assumed optimal nor assumed obsolete. It is the incumbent against which the preservation architecture must compete.

\boxed{
\textbf{Do not optimize the refrigerator.}
}

\boxed{
\textbf{Optimize the preservation service.}
}

\boxed{
\textbf{Let the benchmark determine whether refrigeration remains necessary.}
}

The crucial scientific virtue is that the framework permits the refrigerator to win. That is precisely what makes the alternative result credible if it eventually loses—or wins.

