Appendix A — Viability Theory

A.1 Basic Objects

[Definition A.1.1] Let X = \mathbb{R}^n, U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m compact, and f : X \times U \to X continuous. The set-valued map associated with the controlled dynamics is

F(x) = \{f(x,u) : u \in U\}.

F is Marchaud if it is upper semicontinuous with non-empty compact convex values and linear growth.

[Definition A.1.2] A viable trajectory of F on [0,\tau] starting from x_0 \in K is an absolutely continuous \varphi:[0,\tau]\to K with \varphi(0)=x_0 and \dot\varphi(t) \in F(\varphi(t)) a.e.

[Definition A.1.3] The viability kernel of a closed set K \subseteq X under F is

\operatorname{Viab}_F(K) = \{x_0 \in K : \exists \text{ viable trajectory of } F \text{ in } K, \text{ for all } t \ge 0\}.

A.2 The Viability Theorem (Cited)

[Theorem A.2.1 — Aubin, 1991, Prop. 8] Let F be Marchaud and K \subseteq X closed. Then K is viable under F if and only if

\forall x \in K, \quad F(x) \cap T_K(x) \neq \varnothing. \tag{A.2.1}

Proof. See Aubin (1991), or equivalently the Nagumo–Brezis theorem. The forward direction uses that a viable trajectory \varphi at x has \dot\varphi(0^+) \in T_K(x) by definition of the contingent cone, while \dot\varphi(0^+) \in F(x) by the differential inclusion. The converse uses an Euler construction with steps h\to 0; condition (A.2.1) allows each Euler step to remain in K up to o(h), and Arzelà–Ascoli extracts a convergent subsequence whose limit is viable. \square

[Remark A.2.1] Condition (A.2.1) is the controlled-tangency form of the dissertation's Section 3.1. For the preservation problem, we require, for every z \in K_{\rm service}:

\{f(z,u,\theta,\omega) : u\in U\} \cap T_{K_{\rm service}}(z) \neq \varnothing. \tag{A.2.2}

A.3 Why Compactness Alone Is Insufficient

[Proposition A.3.1 — Non-emptiness is not automatic]
There exist K closed and compact, f Lipschitz, U compact, such that \operatorname{Viab}_F(K) = \varnothing.

Proof. Let X = \mathbb{R}, K = [0,1], U = \{0\}, f(x,u) = 1. Then F(x) = \{1\} for all x. At x = 1, the contingent cone is T_K(1) = (-\infty, 0], so

F(1) \cap T_K(1) = \{1\} \cap (-\infty, 0] = \varnothing.

By Theorem A.2.1, K is not viable, so the viability kernel for the infinite horizon is empty. The finite-horizon version is non-empty on [0,\tau] for \tau \le 1 but empties as \tau \to \infty. \square

[Corollary A.3.1] The dissertation's original claim that "compact K + Lipschitz f + compact U ⇒ \operatorname{Viab}_F(K) \neq \varnothing" is false. Non-emptiness must be verified or assumed.

A.4 Finite-Horizon Viability (Proved)

[Proposition A.4.1 — Finite-horizon viability kernel] Let F be Marchaud, K closed, \tau > 0. Define

\operatorname{Viab}_F^\tau(K) = \{x_0 \in K : \exists \text{ viable trajectory on } [0,\tau] \text{ in } K\}.

Then \operatorname{Viab}_F^\tau(K) is closed. If K is compact and there exists at least one viable trajectory in K of length \tau, then \operatorname{Viab}_F^\tau(K) \neq \varnothing.

Proof. Closedness. Let (x_k) \subset \operatorname{Viab}_F^\tau(K) with x_k \to x. Choose viable trajectories \varphi_k : [0,\tau] \to K with \varphi_k(0) = x_k. By Marchaud regularity, \{\varphi_k\} is equicontinuous and uniformly bounded (linear growth), so Arzelà–Ascoli gives a subsequential limit \varphi with \varphi(0) = x and \varphi(t) \in K (K closed). The limit satisfies the differential inclusion by the closure of F (upper semicontinuity + compact values). Hence x \in \operatorname{Viab}_F^\tau(K).

Non-emptiness. By hypothesis there exists x_0 \in K with at least one viable trajectory on [0,\tau], so x_0 \in \operatorname{Viab}_F^\tau(K). \square

[Corollary A.4.1] For a preservation horizon \tau = 168 h, \operatorname{Viab}_F^\tau(K_{\rm service}) \neq \varnothing is an empirical question, not a consequence of compactness.

