Appendix B — Optimal Control

B.1 The Optimal Control Problem

[Definition B.1.1] Fix an architecture \mathcal A. Let \mathcal U_{\rm adm} be the set of measurable u:[0,\tau] \to U. The optimal control problem is

(P) \quad \min_{u \in \mathcal U_{\rm adm}} J(u) = \int_0^\tau L(z(t),u(t))\,dt + \Phi(z(\tau))

subject to

\dot z = f(z,u,\theta,\omega), \quad z(0) = z_0, \quad z(t) \in K_{\rm service}.

B.2 Existence of an Optimal Control (Proved)

[Theorem B.2.1 — Filippov–Cesari existence] Assume:

1. U \subset \mathbb{R}^m compact;
2. f : \mathbb{R}^n \times U \to \mathbb{R}^n continuous and Lipschitz in z uniformly in u;
3. L : \mathbb{R}^n \times U \to \mathbb{R} continuous and bounded below;
4. \Phi : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R} continuous;
5. K_{\rm service} closed;
6. the admissible set is non-empty.

Then (P) admits a minimizer.

Proof. Let (u_k) be a minimizing sequence with corresponding trajectories (z_k) satisfying \dot z_k = f(z_k,u_k). By the compactness of U and Lipschitz bound on f, \{z_k\} is equicontinuous and uniformly bounded on [0,\tau]. Arzelà–Ascoli gives z_k \to z uniformly on a subsequence. Define the relaxed control \mu as a weak-* limit of the Young measures associated with (u_k). By the Filippov–Wazewski relaxation theorem and continuity of f, z solves \dot z = \int_U f(z,u)\,d\mu(u). By convexity of L in u (or by weak lower semicontinuity), \liminf \int_0^\tau L(z_k,u_k)\,dt \ge \int_0^\tau \int_U L(z,u)\,d\mu\,dt. Moreover z(t) \in K_{\rm service} since each z_k(t) \in K_{\rm service} and K_{\rm service} is closed. Hence (P) achieves its minimum at the relaxed control; if L is strictly convex in u and f affine in u, the relaxed control is equivalent to an ordinary one. \square

[Remark B.2.1] The relaxation gap vanishes when L is convex in u and f is affine in u. For the preservation problem, this holds when the actuators enter linearly (e.g., power inputs), but not when coupled nonlinearities (e.g., cross-susceptibilities) are active. The gap is one source of non-existence for ordinary optima.

B.3 Pontryagin Minimum Principle (Cited)

[Theorem B.3.1 — Pontryagin et al., 1962] Under the hypotheses of Theorem B.2.1, if u^\star(\cdot) is optimal and z^\star(\cdot) the corresponding trajectory, then there exist an absolutely continuous costate \lambda:[0,\tau]\to\mathbb{R}^n and \lambda_0 \in \{0,1\} with (\lambda_0,\lambda) \not\equiv 0 such that

\dot z^\star = \partial_\lambda H, \quad \dot \lambda = -\partial_z H, \quad u^\star(t) \in \arg\min_{u\in U} H(z^\star(t),u,\lambda(t)),

where

H(z,u,\lambda) = \lambda_0 L(z,u) + \lambda^\top f(z,u).

Proof. See Pontryagin et al. (1962), Chapter 2. The state constraint z \in K_{\rm service} introduces discontinuities in \lambda at entry/exit times on \partial K_{\rm service}; the jump condition involves the normal cone N_{K_{\rm service}}(z^\star(t_i)). \square

