Appendix C — Pareto Existence

C.1 Dominance and the Pareto Frontier

[Definition C.1.1] For a cost vector \mathbf J : \mathfrak A \to \mathbb{R}^p with all components in a lower-is-better convention,

\mathcal A_1 \prec \mathcal A_2 \iff J_i(\mathcal A_1) \le J_i(\mathcal A_2) \; \forall i \in \{1,\dots,p\}, \quad \exists j : J_j(\mathcal A_1) < J_j(\mathcal A_2).

[Definition C.1.2] The Pareto frontier is

\mathcal P^\star = \{\mathcal A \in \mathfrak A : \nexists \mathcal B \in \mathfrak A, \; \mathcal B \prec \mathcal A\}.

C.2 Existence for a Finite Catalogue (Proved)

[Theorem C.2.1] If \mathfrak A is finite and non-empty, then \mathcal P^\star \neq \varnothing.

Proof. Suppose \mathcal P^\star = \varnothing. Then every \mathcal A \in \mathfrak A is dominated by some \mathcal B \in \mathfrak A. Choose \mathcal A_1 \in \mathfrak A. By hypothesis there is \mathcal A_2 \prec \mathcal A_1. Iterating, we obtain a strictly \prec-descending sequence

\mathcal A_1 \succ \mathcal A_2 \succ \mathcal A_3 \succ \cdots

in a finite set. Since \prec is a strict partial order (irreflexive, transitive, asymmetric), no element can appear twice; otherwise a cycle would contradict asymmetry. Hence the sequence is infinite, contradicting finiteness of \mathfrak A. \square

C.3 Existence for a Compact Space (Proved)

[Theorem C.3.1] If \mathfrak A is compact (in the Vietoris topology or any topology in which \mathbf J is continuous) and \mathbf J : \mathfrak A \to \mathbb{R}^p is continuous, then \mathcal P^\star \neq \varnothing.

Proof. The image \mathbf J(\mathfrak A) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p is compact. Consider the partial order \le on \mathbb{R}^p given by componentwise inequality. The set \mathbf J(\mathfrak A) is a non-empty compact subset of the partially ordered set (\mathbb{R}^p, \le) that is order-complete (every chain has a lower bound given by its componentwise infimum, which is in the closure of the compact set). By Zorn's lemma, \mathbf J(\mathfrak A) admits a minimal element \mathbf J^\star. By definition, \mathbf J^\star is the image of some \mathcal A^\star \in \mathfrak A, which by minimality is in \mathcal P^\star. \square

[Remark C.3.1] The original proof in the dissertation ("contains at least the minimizers of each individual objective") is incorrect. A minimizer of J_i alone can be dominated by a point that improves J_j while leaving J_i unchanged. The correct argument uses compactness of the image and Zorn's lemma (or scalarization: for every \lambda > 0 in \mathbb{R}^p, the minimizer of \lambda^\top \mathbf J is in \mathcal P^\star, and its existence follows from compactness).

C.4 The Architecture Space Is Not Compact as Written

[Proposition C.4.1] The architecture space \mathfrak A_{\rm feasible} defined in Version 4 is not compact in any standard topology.

Proof. \mathfrak A_{\rm feasible} contains the set of all admissible graphs G on a countable vertex set (topologies of component interconnection). This set is infinite and, in the Vietoris topology, not compact because the discrete metric on a countable set is not totally bounded. Hence \mathfrak A_{\rm feasible} is not compact. \square

[Corollary C.4.1] Theorem C.3.1 does not directly apply. For the benchmark, we therefore restrict to a finite catalogue \mathfrak A_{\rm finite} \subset \mathfrak A_{\rm feasible} (Theorem C.2.1 applies), or to a compactified design space (e.g., bounded graph degree and fixed vertex count).

