Appendix D — Multicaloric Formalism

D.1 Second-Order Entropy Expansion

[Proposition D.1.1 — Taylor expansion] Let S(T,X_1,\dots,X_k) be C^2 in a neighborhood of (T_0,X_1^0,\dots,X_k^0). Then

\Delta S = \sum_i \left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial X_i}\right)_{T,X_{j\neq i}} \Delta X_i + \frac12 \sum_{i,j} \left(\frac{\partial^2 S}{\partial X_i\partial X_j}\right)_T \Delta X_i \Delta X_j + O(\|\Delta X\|^3).

Proof. Standard multivariable Taylor theorem at order 2. \square

[Definition D.1.2 — Cross-susceptibility] The cross-susceptibility between fields X_i and X_j is

\chi_{ij} = \left(\frac{\partial^2 S}{\partial X_i \partial X_j}\right)_T.

D.2 Coupling ≠ Synergy

[Proposition D.2.1 — Sign indeterminacy] There exists no general theorem asserting \chi_{12} \Delta X_1 \Delta X_2 > 0. In particular, both signs are realizable in physical systems.

Proof. Consider the Landau expansion F(T,M,P) = \frac{a(T)}{2}M^2 + \frac{b}{4}M^4 + \frac{c}{2}P^2 - \kappa M P with a(T) = a_0 (T - T_c), b>0, c>0, \kappa>0. Then

S(T,M,P) = -\partial_T F = -\frac{a_0}{2}M^2 + \text{const.}, \qquad \chi_{MP} = \partial^2_{MP} S = 0,

so the cross-term is identically zero at leading order in this model. Add a coupling -\gamma M^2 P^2 to obtain \chi_{MP} = -2\gamma, which is negative. Change the sign of \gamma to obtain a positive cross-term. Hence the sign of \chi_{12} is not universal. \square

[Corollary D.2.1] The original "Theorem 3.2.1 (Multicaloric Advantage)" is not a theorem. It is a definition of a physical synergy condition whose sign must be established empirically for each material system.

D.3 System-Level Synergy

[Definition D.3.1] For two architectures \mathcal A_i,\mathcal A_j and their coupled architecture \mathcal A_{ij}, the system-level synergy is

\Delta J_{\rm sys}(\mathcal A_i,\mathcal A_j) = J(\mathcal A_i) + J(\mathcal A_j) - J(\mathcal A_{ij}).

[Remark D.3.1] \Delta J_{\rm sys} has no direct precedent in the reviewed literature; it is a proposed metric of this dissertation. The 2025 Phys. Rev. B experimental work validates the physical cross-term but does not define a system-level cost synergy.

