Appendix E — Exergy Analysis

E.1 The Gouy–Stodola Theorem (Cited)

[Theorem E.1.1 — Gouy, 1889; Stodola, 1898] For any thermodynamic system in contact with a reference environment at temperature T_0, the rate of exergy destruction is

\dot E_{x,D} = T_0 \dot S_{\rm gen},

where \dot S_{\rm gen} \ge 0 is the total entropy generation rate.

Proof. See Bejan (2016), Advanced Engineering Thermodynamics, §3.6. Combination of the first and second laws applied to the system and its environment. \square

E.2 Exergy Balance

[Proposition E.2.1 — Exergy balance] For a control volume V with boundary \partial V,

\frac{dE_x}{dt} = \dot E_{x,\rm in} - \dot E_{x,\rm out} - T_0 \dot S_{\rm gen}.

Proof. Apply the first law \dot E = \dot Q - \dot W + \sum_j \dot n_j h_j and second law \dot S = \dot Q/T_0 + \dot S_{\rm gen} relative to the dead state R_0. Multiplying the second law by T_0 and subtracting from the first yields the result, after identifying E_x = E - E_0 - T_0(S - S_0) + p_0(V - V_0) - \sum_j n_j (\mu_{j,0} - \mu_{j,0}). \square

E.3 Reference Environment

[Definition E.3.1] The dead state is

R_0 = (T_0, p_0, \mathbf y_0^{\rm env}), \quad T_0 = 298.15\,\mathrm{K}, \quad p_0 = 101.325\,\mathrm{kPa},

with \mathbf y_0^{\rm env} the standard dry-air composition (78.084 % N₂, 20.946 % O₂, 0.934 % Ar, 0.042 % CO₂) at RH₀ = 50 %.

[Remark E.3.1] Fixing R_0 is standard practice in exergy analysis. The precise value of \mathbf y_0^{\rm env} for preservation benchmarks is a proposed specification of this dissertation.

