Appendix F — Chance-Constrained Feasibility

F.1 The Chance-Constrained Problem

[Definition F.1.1] For a policy \pi and a joint distribution \mathbb P on (\theta,\omega),

P_{\rm fail}(\pi) = \mathbb P\!\left( \exists t \in [0,\tau] : z(t) \notin K_{\rm service} \right).

The chance constraint is P_{\rm fail}(\pi) \le \epsilon.

F.2 Scenario Convex Approximation (Cited)

[Theorem F.2.1 — Campi–Garatti, 2008] Let \pi \mapsto P_{\rm fail}(\pi) be the violation probability of a chance-constrained program whose constraint set is convex in the decision variable \pi, and let \{\xi^{(1)},\dots,\xi^{(N)}\} be N independent samples from \mathbb P. Let \pi^\star_N be the optimizer of the scenario problem (all N constraints enforced). Then for every \epsilon \in (0,1),

\mathbb P^N\!\left( P_{\rm fail}(\pi^\star_N) \le \epsilon \right) \ge 1 - \sum_{k=0}^{p-1} \binom{N}{k} \epsilon^k (1-\epsilon)^{N-k}.

Proof. See Campi & Garatti (2008), Mathematical Programming 115, 219–240, Theorem 1. The bound is the Kolmogorov–Smirnov tail of the binomial distribution. \square

[Corollary F.2.1] For p decision variables and \epsilon = 0.01, the required sample size is approximately N \approx 700\,p. The sample size must be pre-registered.

F.3 Separation from Viability

[Proposition F.3.1] *The deterministic tangency condition (A.2.2) is neither necessary nor sufficient for \(P_{\rm fail}(\pi) \le \epsilon$ for $\epsilon \in (0,1)$.*

Proof sketch. Consider a system with F(x) \cap T_K(x) \ne \varnothing at every x \in K under the nominal parameters \theta_0, \omega_0, but where a perturbation \theta reverses the sign of the drift at a single boundary point with probability 1/2. Then tangency holds at \theta_0, but P_{\rm fail} \ge 1/2, so the chance constraint fails for \epsilon = 0.01. Conversely, if a policy is feasible for the chance constraint, the nominal trajectory it generates need not satisfy tangency at every boundary point (it may avoid those points). \square

