Appendix G — Technology Maturity Formalism

G.1 The Maturity Filter

[Definition G.1.1] Let \mathrm{TRL} : \mathfrak A \to \{1,\dots,9\} assign a Technology Readiness Level to each architecture. The maturity-admissible set is

\mathfrak A_{\rm maturity} = \{\mathcal A \in \mathfrak A : \mathrm{TRL}(\mathcal A) \ge \mathrm{TRL}_{\min}\}.

[Proposition G.1.1 — Filter is non-trivial] There exist architectures in \mathfrak A_{\rm feasible} with \mathrm{TRL}(\mathcal A) < \mathrm{TRL}_{\min}.

Proof. By the TRL scale (NASA/ESA), TRL 1–3 correspond to basic principles, TRL 4–6 to lab-to-pilot validation, and TRL 7–9 to prototype-to-deployment. The reviewed literature records magnetic cooling at TRL 3–4 in most configurations and TRL 6–9 in one commercial line. Since TRL is non-constant across \mathfrak A_{\rm feasible}, the filter \mathfrak A_{\rm maturity} \subsetneq \mathfrak A_{\rm feasible} is proper. \square

G.2 No Cross-Level Comparison

[Proposition G.2.1] *A material-level COP and a device-level COP are not comparable, even if both are in units of \(\mathrm{COP}$.*

Proof. A material-level COP is defined as \mathrm{COP}_{\rm mat} = Q_{\rm mat}/W_{\rm mat} where Q_{\rm mat} is the cooling produced by the material per cycle and W_{\rm mat} is the work input to the material. A device-level COP is \mathrm{COP}_{\rm dev} = Q_{\rm dev}/W_{\rm dev} with Q_{\rm dev}, W_{\rm dev} measured at the device boundary. These are related by

\mathrm{COP}_{\rm dev} = \eta_{\rm regen} \cdot \eta_{\rm HT} \cdot \eta_{\rm par} \cdot \mathrm{COP}_{\rm mat},

where \eta_{\rm regen}, \eta_{\rm HT}, \eta_{\rm par} are the regeneration, heat-transfer, and parasitic-loss efficiencies, each < 1. Hence \mathrm{COP}_{\rm dev} < \mathrm{COP}_{\rm mat}, and a numerical comparison between the two is meaningless without specifying the conversion factors. \square

