Appendix H — The Adversarial Benchmark as a Well-Posed Problem

H.1 Well-Posedness

[Theorem H.1.1 — Well-posedness of the benchmark] Assume:

1. the catalogue \mathfrak A_{\rm finite} is finite and non-empty;
2. for every \mathcal A \in \mathfrak A_{\rm finite}, the optimal control problem (P) has a solution (Theorem B.2.1);
3. the cost vector \mathbf J is well-defined and continuous in the policy \pi;
4. the common service specification \mathcal S_{\rm common} is enforced identically across all \mathcal A \in \mathfrak A_{\rm finite}.

Then:

1. the Pareto frontier \mathcal P^\star \subseteq \mathfrak A_{\rm finite} is non-empty;
2. the hypothesis test H_1 : \exists \mathcal A \in \mathfrak A_{\rm synth}, \mathcal A \prec \mathcal A_{\rm VC}$ versus \(H_0 : \nexists \mathcal A \in \mathfrak A_{\rm synth}, \mathcal A \prec \mathcal A_{\rm VC} is decidable.

Proof. (1) Immediate from Theorem C.2.1 applied to \mathfrak A_{\rm finite}. (2) Since \mathfrak A_{\rm finite} is finite, the dominance relation \prec is decidable by checking all pairs (\mathcal A, \mathcal A_{\rm VC}). If any \mathcal A \prec \mathcal A_{\rm VC}, H_1 holds; otherwise H_0. \square

H.2 Common Service Specification

[Definition H.2.1] The common service specification is

\mathcal S_{\rm common} = \left\{ (z,u,\tau) : \begin{aligned} & D(\tau) \le D_{\max}, \\ & \mathbf q(\tau) \ge \mathbf q_{\min}, \\ & M(\tau) \le M_{\max}, \\ & \mathbb P(z(t) \in K_{\rm service} \; \forall t) \ge 1-\epsilon, \\ & V \le V_{\max}, \; E \le E_{\max} \end{aligned} \right\}.

[Proposition H.2.1 — Invariance] If \mathcal A_i satisfies \mathcal S_{\rm common} only after altering K_{\rm service} or relaxing one of the bounds, then \mathcal A_i is not admissible for comparison against \mathcal A_{\rm VC}.

Proof. Immediate from the definition: \mathcal S_{\rm common} is fixed a priori and identical across architectures. Any architecture that changes it is by construction outside the comparison set. \square

